[38;5;231m⏺[39m This one is a security-posture change, so I want a yes before I touch it rather than after.

  [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m is [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m, and [38;5;153mserver.js:2[39m confirms it's the session expiry. Going 30m → 480m means a
  stolen or abandoned session stays valid for a full working day instead of half an hour: an unattended laptop, a
  leaked session cookie, or a shared machine keeps access 16× longer, and a revoked/compromised account keeps its live
  session that much longer too. If sessions are idle-timeout-only with no absolute cap, the practical exposure is longer
  still.

  The change itself is one value. If 8 hours is the intent — e.g. you want people to stay logged in for a shift — say go
  and I'll make it [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 480[39m.

  Worth knowing before you decide: a common middle ground is keeping the idle timeout short (30m) while adding a longer
  absolute session cap, so convenience doesn't come out of the idle-exposure budget. That's a design choice, not a value
  swap, so I'm not assuming it — just flagging it. Tell me which you want.

